i've been sent a bunch of questions from  a lovely group of very young students so  i'm going to answer them all in one hit  here in this video so the first question  says how did you find your passion for  maths  i think to answer that question there's  sort of two pieces of the puzzle the  first one is that i have an older  brother and he always was very gifted at  maths and loved maths from a much  younger age than me and so when i was  growing up when i was in primary school  even though maths was not a subject  which i was particularly gifted at i  watched my brother do things  particularly he would program  mathematical things into a computer and  i would watch him with interest and say  like what's going on there i was always  very curious even though i didn't  understand it at the time so i'd say  that my brother kind of planted a seed  there of mathematical curiosity and then  when i became a teacher i started to  realize that actually helping people  learn things and making this idea  click and make sense in someone's mind  that's something that i absolutely fell  in love with so i think that's part of  why i enjoy math so much because that  happens all the time when i'm teaching  it how hard is it to teach kids and  what's it like to be a teacher  it can be very challenging to teach kids  in a sense because everything that we're  trying to do when we're teaching is  meant to be something that is you know  you're trying to put your mind into a  sort of  mental acrobatics to wrap its head and  wrap your head around a concept or an  idea or a skill that you've never done  before and so by the nature of it  because you're doing that and the brain  is you know in some ways uncomfortable  when it's trying to do something new  like that that is challenging but at the  same time we're kind of built to learn  you know from the first moment that we  are born we're looking at the world  we're taking insights and sounds and as  we learn to speak and write and interact  with human beings and people around us  these are all things which our brains  are kind of built to do so in that sense  learning is kind of an easy thing it's a  natural thing and so even though it can  be hard i really enjoy doing it  as to what it's like to be a teacher i  think it's full of like i said the joy  of trying to work out what's a great way  to explain something or what's just a  new perspective on an old idea uh it can  be challenging trying to juggle all of  the different kinds of tasks you know  from 9 a.m or whenever your school  starts to 3 pm or whenever your school  ends there's all the parts of being a  teacher that you as a student might see  but there's all kinds of other bits as  well every lesson that i prepare takes  me sometimes hours outside of that  lesson time to think through and to plan  and then there is you know setting tasks  activities exams and then the marking of  those and then things like writing  reports and other sorts of organization  that happen behind the scenes as a  teacher they can keep us pretty busy but  they're all important to help students  learn so that's why it's worth doing  them what path did you take to get where  you are now this is a very simple  question that i feel like has a very  complicated answer it was a very winding  path in a way but i went to university  to train to become a teacher that was  the university of sydney and in some  ways the most automatic thing to do  after that was then to start working in  a school and that's what i did for let's  see six years i taught mathematics and  computing studies as a regular classroom  teacher but then i learned that  after i've been teaching for a little  while and i got a sense of how to do the  job and i i sort of became comfortable  with how to do it and i wasn't just very  confused and learning new things all the  time i started to realize okay i would  like to be i think i have the  opportunity to serve other people by  leading other teachers and supporting  them so that's when i became a head  teacher of mathematics when i joined my  current school cherrywood technology  high school and that was also around the  same time that i started filming my  classroom lessons i didn't have a fancy  camera like the one that i'm looking at  right now i just used my phone and even  though that was a simple act and there  were no grand plans behind it i was just  trying to help a student who was sick  and couldn't get to school and be in our  lessons  even though that was a very simple thing  it kind of opened up an enormous world  that i didn't expect because people  could get to view my teaching from  anywhere in the world just as long as  they had an internet connection and a  device it sort of  helped many people encounter me and get  to know the way that i love to teach and  learn  way more than i would just have in the  regular classroom so that was almost 10  years ago now that i started doing that  and i think that that's what opened the  door to um you know hosting tv shows and  writing books and now working across uh  the new south wales department of  education and supporting teachers all  over the state and even beyond that  sometimes around the country and i've  even had the privilege of going overseas  and working with teachers in other  countries so it's a bit of a winding  path i don't know that i can say like oh  i if i'd planned it this is the way that  it would have worked out a lot of things  happened that um i could never have  expected i didn't know that one and a  half million people would start  subscribing to my youtube channel that  was definitely not part of the plan but  i was delighted that it happened so i  guess the lesson there  is to be  ready for opportunities  to be thinking of ways that you can help  people um and also you know being open  to new thoughts and and trying new  things innovating even if it's a bit  risky um and trying to see what happens  when we do those things to help others  do you prefer teaching primary school  kids more than high school kids in  mathematics i feel like this question  has a little bit of bias in it i love  teaching all  people of all ages and that's not just  me sitting on the fence i don't just  like teaching primary school in high  school i love teaching beyond that in  people who've left school maybe they're  at university i love talking to adults  you know like i said before people who  are also teachers like me or parents or  people you know even beyond that i  really truly love teaching everyone and  there's a different challenge along with  all the different age groups all the  different kinds of people that you teach  i will say i find it harder to teach  primary school kids rather than high  school kids because i was trained how to  teach the high school mathematics  curriculum and the the primary  mathematics curriculum and the syllabus  and all the concepts and ideas there  they're things that i've picked up along  the way you might say well eddie you  actually went to primary school you did  all that maths isn't it easy and i would  say no it's not  any more than being a patient in a  hospital equips you to be a doctor or  eating a meal equips you to be a chef  there's all kinds of knowledge and  skills sort of hiding in the background  or hidden underneath that isn't obvious  when you're learning it it's only sort  of becomes apparent when you're teaching  it so something as simple as say  division i remember the first time that  i was trying to teach  dividing to a group of very young  students and in high school if you say  something like 24 divided by three you  expect the answer just to be eight full  stop that's it end of story but in fact  24 divided by three can mean  how big are the groups if you  divide up 24 into three even groups so  you would have eight things in each  group and that's why the answer is eight  but in the same way you could actually  think of 24 divided by three as  how many groups would you get if you  divided 24 into groups of three so you'd  have a three here three here three here  how many threes would you have and the  answer is eight so those two things are  called partition and quotation and i  didn't know that they existed back when  i was learning to be a teacher at  university and even as a high school  teacher so i find that more challenging  but i do enjoy it because i like  learning things and so teaching primary  school kids gives me the opportunity to  learn even more stuff  who is the teacher who inspired you to  get to the place you are now  i cannot pick out a single teacher who  was the inspiration who helped me to get  to where i am now and achieve the things  that i've achieved and that's because i  had so many wonderful teachers who over  the years sort of contributed each of  the little bits to my story that make me  the person who i am now so for example i  could talk about mrs yates who was my  drama teacher i did drama from year nine  all the way through to year 12 and mrs  yates had me for the hsc which my final  years of school  she taught me the value of taking on a  persona and being able to sort of convey  another person's perspective that isn't  just your own or i could talk about  mr mcfadyen who taught me english for  four years and explained to me the power  of stories and storytelling is something  that i do in every single lesson that i  teach even though the subject that i  teach is mathematics i could talk about  mr brown my agriculture teacher who  taught me the importance of treating  students as not just people who are  supposed to learn in your class but as  fellow human beings with incredible  dignity and value or i could talk about  mr best my music teacher  who taught me the lesson of patience i  think music teachers are amazing because  they are so gifted at music and they can  often play multiple instruments but they  have to have incredible patience because  they're dealing with people who do not  know what to do with their instruments  at all and they sound terrible  especially to begin with and then they  will develop into skill so every single  one of those teachers that i just named  and many more besides they each were  inspirations to me and i'm just very  grateful to have had many of them who've  mentored me and continue to teach me  what it means to be a great teacher  how do you make maths look easy for kids  who find it hard  this is an interesting question because  i don't find maths easy i find it a  subject which even though i teach it  i've always found it something uh  intellectually you know it's it's full  of hard thinking and sort of getting  things wrong and then trying again and  learning lessons so i'm glad some people  think that i make it look easy um i  guess what i would say is firstly what i  just mentioned the fact that i can  empathize with people when they find  that's hard to learn because i found  maths hard to learn and i still do in  many ways i think that empathy is very  important because there's nothing um  more discouraging than when someone says  to you oh it's very obvious why don't  you understand i think i understand and  everyone else understands why don't you  i've never been like that in my classes  because i don't find things easy to  understand i have to work really hard at  them so that's one thing secondly i  would say  connecting it to knowledge that we  already possess i think is very  important if you started reading a book  and you started reading instead of at  the beginning if you started reading in  the middle of course you would find it  confusing because you don't know who any  of the characters are there's like all  these kinds of events that have happened  leading up to the thing you're reading  about but you don't know what any of  those events  were or what they mean and so you'll  kind of be thinking what does it signify  what's the importance of this event  happening i don't know because i don't  have any of the background knowledge so  often we learn mathematics and we kind  of start in the middle not intentionally  but maybe there were things from earlier  on maybe we were sick or maybe we  changed from one school to another there  can be any number of reasons why we're  missing as it were earlier chapters in  the story of mathematics that we're  learning so it's hardly a surprise then  as we progress further we might think oh  i'm a bit confused i've got gaps in my  understanding and i'm trying to build to  change metaphors on a sort of wobbly  foundation and that can lead to  confusion so i guess um to try and help  mathematics learning be easier rather  than harder it's important to be able to  identify like part of my job is to not  just tell students and convey ideas but  to actually  learn from students and listen and say  what is it that you already know what  are the things you don't know that  actually are important to the things  we're learning today if i can identify  what those are  and address any weaknesses or gaps that  you have that will help you get a firmer  grip on the new ideas concepts and  skills that we're learning  so thank you for those questions i hope  you found those answers helpful all the  best in your mathematical learning  journey i hope i get to meet you one day

